Artemis the Ranger wrote:

> I mean a good, old, razzle-dazzle, abracadabra,
> sleight-of-hand stage magician. The Magician would be expert at doing
> things like finding and escaping from traps (only one turn required, a
> la Houndini), getting away quickly from tough fights, etc.

I'd call it an illusionist, not a magician.

> Races: Human, elf

We should have hobbits as player classes...

> Extremely high charisma and dexterity.
> High intelligence.
> Moderate wisdom and constitution.
> Extremely low strength.

Contortionists usually are quite strong, but I guess you cannot have
*all* stats be above average. Why not an even lower wisdom, for balance?

> Intrinics:
> - speed (XL 4)

Why?

> - searching (XL6)

Guess so, although being able to hide things easily does not imply that
you're also good at spotting them.

> - instant escape from all traps (XL10)

I like this: it's distinctive from other classes. Of course, sleeping
gas, fire, etc. would still work the same. Or are you suggesting you'd
escape those as well? That I would sooner connect to the rogue class.

> - intrinsic teleport (XL16) (so the Magician can just...vanish if
> he/she gets into trouble.)

Sure. You might even consider teleport control at an even higher level.
That might be an extra incentive no to try to stay at level 14.

> Restricted in "heavy" weapons (long sword, hammer, two-handed sword,
> two-weapon combat, etc.) Unrestricted with light and ranged weaponry.
> Restricted in all spell classes except escape (for getting away
> quickly) and enchantment (goes with high charisma). (This restriction
> is intended to stop the Magician and Wizard classes from overlapping
> too much.)

> Starting inventory:
> A +2 baton (those little fake "wands" magicians carry. Equivalent to a
> "lighter" quarterstaff for game purposes.)

Call it a "cane", I guess. That's used to hit people, sometimes, as
opposed to the baton (which I associate with an orchestra director, not
with an illusionist).

> One of either: cloak of displacement, cloak of invisibility, boots of
> speed, or gauntlets of dexterity, enchanted to +2.

I'm not sure whether this may be too strong, even if the wizard starts
with a lot better equipment. But comparing this to, say, a barbarian or
rogue makes it seem a bit strong.

> A +0 top hat (useless as armour, but when a)pplied acts as a bag of
> tricks - i.e. "pulling a rabbit out of a hat.")

There should be a limited number of charges to this. It's like starting
with a bag of tricks. Writing this, I think that a bag of tricks may be
just as appropriate (again, with a limited number of charges).
Illusionists often pull things out of bags as well, and the epitheton
"of tricks" fits nicely with the class.

> 1 random ring drawn from the following: teleportation, teleport
> control, steath, invisibility (unless you got the cloak already).

Why unless you got the cloak already? Not every character should start
overly strong. And why stealth? Perhaps you might give that as a
starting intrinsic, closely connected to the illusionist's ability to
sneak away from the stage without being seen even by those who are
actually looking for her.

> 4 random objects drawn from the following: scroll of confuse monster
> (since magicians are *really* good at confusing people), scroll of
> light, scroll of taming, potion of confusion, potion of hallucination,
> potion of invisibility, potion of levitation, potion of speed, wand of
> light, wand of opening, wand of locking, wand of make invisible, wand
> of secret door detection, wand of teleport.

Even more magical objects? Well, perhaps it makes sense, since the
illusionist uses objects to do his tricks, not real magic.

> Silk scarf (blindfold).

This is a *very* powerful item for starters, since it allows you to
attack floating eyes without danger, and after that, to use telepathy
(if a corpse is left).

> 50% chance of a crystal ball, magic whistle, magic harp, or magic
> flute.

Even more magic objects? I think this will be too strong.

> Quest Leader: Houndini

Of course.

> Quest artifact: The Magic Bag (artifact bag of holding). When carried
> it confers magic resistance and acts as a normal bag of holding. When
> #invoked it acts as a limited wand of wishing (with major
> restrictions): you are asked what you want to wish for, and "the bag
> opens to reveal a <foo>."

That would be *way* too strong. A bag of holding in itself is powerful
enough, even without the wishing. Make the invokation power a lot less
useful, and it's still good.

> Restrictions: objects must fit inside a standard bag,

All objects except boulders and chests of the various sorts do, if I
recall correctly.

> be randomly generated,

How would this be wishing if an object is randomly generated?

I could envision something like wishing for a CLASS of objects (rings,
tools, stones, ...), whereupon a random non-magical object from that
class is chosen (if no non-magical objects exist in that class, nothing
comes out of the bag.

It would be a sort of bag of tricks for objects (the ADND bag of many
things would be comparable).

> and weapons/armor/tools must be non-magical.

That would be a minimal restriction.

> You cannot wish for artifacts, multiple items, enchanted
> weapons/armor, or wands of wishing or polymorph. In addition, the
> greater the value of the item (in zorkmids), the longer the invocation
> timeout.

I like the variable timeout.

> Any ideas for gods or quest nemesis?

Nemesis: a tiger (think ... and Roy, I forgot the first one's first
name).

Gods: really no idea. That's a problem when you try to make up a
non-mythological class, with no references in fantasy literature either.

Also, you still have to design the Quest levels (not too much alike
those of the Rogue, please).

